.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Unlike the static Matplotlib plots elsewhere in physicskit.classical.visualizers, these are draggable/zoomable in a live Python session or a browser -- useful for directly inspecting how close a rigid-body trajectory passes to the unstable intermediate-axis pole, or for zooming into a precessing orbit&#x27;s perihelion. The two systems are governed by the equations already introduced elsewhere in the gallery: EulerTop&#x27;s torque-free Euler equations, I_i\dot\omega_i = (I_j - I_k)\,\omega_j\omega_k (cyclic in i,j,k) for the body-frame angular velocity (see /api/gallery/classical/rotations/plot_01_dzhanibekov), and KeplerSystem&#x27;s separable Hamiltonian H = |p|^2/(2\mu) + V(r) with the post-Newtonian perturbation c_\mathrm{pn}/r^3 in V(r) that drives the perihelion precession (see /api/gallery/classical/newtonian/plot_02_kepler_precession).">

